{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Esav took his wives, his sons, and his daughters</b> — this journey was after his brother returned from Haran and took hold of the land of Canaan, as it says here; and it is possible that it was also after their father's death. Now, while his brother was still in Haran, Esav was in Se'ir, as is written above (Genesis 32:4), but the matter is that Esav went to Se'ir in the days of the chiefs of the Horite, the inhabitants of the land, and became a chieftain going with four hundred men, while his sons and his household were in the land of Canaan. And it is possible that he had an estate there in one place in the plain, not in the mountain of Se'ir; therefore Scripture ascribes to him \"to the land of Se'ir, the field of Edom.\" And after his brother returned to the land of Canaan, he turned away from before him, for he knew that the land of Canaan was his brother's inheritance, which his father had given him in his blessing. And he took his sons and all the persons of his household, a great company, and went to Se'ir to settle there. Then he made war against the sons of Se'ir the Horite, the inhabitants of the land, for perhaps they feared him and would not let him enter their territory in the mountain of Se'ir where the fortresses were, but rather he should dwell in the field of Edom in his former place. \"And HaShem destroyed them from before them, and they dispossessed them and dwelt in their stead,\" as stated in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 2:21). Therefore it says there (Deuteronomy 2:5), \"For as a possession to Esav I have given Mount Se'ir.\"" "And the meaning of <b>and he went to the land</b> — Onkelos rendered (Onkelos on Genesis 36:6), \"to another land.\" And Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 36:6), \"to sojourn wherever he may find\" — that he went to a land where he would find a place to encamp, until he came to Mount Se'ir and settled there. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it says \"and he went to the land of Se'ir,\" and the specifier was omitted because it is understood, since Scripture had already mentioned (Genesis 32:4) that he was standing in the land of Se'ir. And is it not known that it was there he would bring his household? And it states shortly afterward (Genesis 36:8), \"And Esav dwelt in Mount Se'ir.\" This is like (I Kings 16:18) \"And he came to the citadel of the king's house and burned over it the house of the king\" — its measurement is \"the king's house,\" the one that was in it. And likewise, \"and he burned the high place, crushing it to dust, and burned the Asherah\" (II Kings 23:15) — meaning the one belonging to Yarov'am that is mentioned in that place. And likewise, \"Yo'av said to the Kushite, 'Go, tell the king,' and the Kushite bowed to Yo'av\" (II Samuel 18:21); \"and the donkey and the lion\" (I Kings 13:28); and likewise, \"for the ships were broken at Etzyon Gever\" (ibid. 22:49) — the ships. And there are many such examples. So too here: \"and he went to the land\" — like \"to the land,\" the land of Se'ir that is mentioned."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:31.735661607Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}